Richard Bos wrote:
> Ssulian <wichitajayhawks@msn.com> wrote:
>
>> Is it a common strategy to engineer bones levels?
>
> On the contrary, it is considered cheating.

I think there was a 10+ challenge ascension post that used engineered
bones files.  I recall folks calling it something like bones scumming.

Dying just to leave a bones file seems very wasteful of effort to me. 
Both generating a bones file and using an existing bones file are random
IIRC - My PC has several bones files for levels I have explored in
several new games.

On the other hand shuttling the main items of your stash down before
exploring a level isn't that bad a strategy as long as you are not in a
hurry.  I often keep one of more main stashes that get most of the items
I have encountered before plus a smaller point stash that I shuttle down
with me as I explore each level.  I keep an assortment of backup items
in the bag so such a small point stash would have a larger assortment of
backup and infrequent use type items.  I take it this well of shuttling
a stash down is less than what the OP means.

To some extent a bones file can be viewed as storing the effort that
went into such a smaller stash.  If I get dragon scale mail early in a
game then die to stupidity and/or typo it's nice that a later game I may
get further use of that armor.

But in the end no items will give an ascension.  Ascensions come from
playing skill not from items.  Folks doing orc racial ascensions
demonstrate that fact very vividly.


